Subject: Lower debugger traps, and name the limits that cannot be lowered

What this does
  Lower debugger traps, and name the limits that cannot be lowered.

Why we need it
  `OP_esc1`'s debug sub-operations -- every `debugger;`, and every statement trap a parse-time handler emits -- had no register lowering, so a function containing one failed closed with "function was not lowered" at call time; a call with more than 255 arguments, which is legal JavaScript, hit the same message, which names nothing.

What it adds
  New cold opcode `ROP_debug` (operand: the `JS_DEBUG_TRACE_*` flags byte) captures the ip and calls the same hook the stack VM's `OP_esc1` calls, inert with no handler; a function carrying a trap keeps the frame authoritative (slot promotion, block-local read forwarding and `regvm_fold_frame_reads` are all off for it, so the debugger reads and writes the live slots); the argument-count and object-field limits throw a compile-time `InternalError` naming the limit.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  Yes. Regenerated register blobs are in `9999`; `BC_VERSION` is unchanged.
